WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.020
Filling one cell at a time is slow.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.661
Myers's bit-vector algorithm, from 1999

3
00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:09.130
fills a whole column at once.

4
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.196
It starts from one fact

5
00:00:11.196 --> 00:00:14.200
neighbouring cells differ by at most one.

6
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.692
So a column can be stored as

7
00:00:16.768 --> 00:00:19.827
the steps going down it: plus one, zero

8
00:00:19.827 --> 00:00:21.510
or minus one per row.

9
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.940
The first target column of

10
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.540
our example goes one, zero, one, two, three.

11
00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.848
Its steps are minus one

12
00:00:29.848 --> 00:00:32.050
then plus one three times.

13
00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.590
Myers keeps these steps in two bit vectors.

14
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.198
One, called P V

15
00:00:37.198 --> 00:00:40.580
marks the rows where the step is plus one.

16
00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.153
The other, M V

17
00:00:42.153 --> 00:00:45.250
marks the rows where it is minus one.

18
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.580
Masks say, for each letter

19
00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.000
which rows of the query hold it.

20
00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:52.744
The next column comes from the current one

21
00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:54.910
with a handful of bitwise operations.

22
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.891
A machine word holds sixty-four rows

23
00:00:57.891 --> 00:01:01.020
so each operation handles sixty-four cells.

24
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.520
The trick is one addition.

25
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.030
It carries a bit down a run of rows

26
00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.130
just as a chain of matches carries

27
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.870
a low value down a diagonal.

28
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.700
Here are the four columns of our example.

29
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.467
The bottom cell goes three, two, two, one

30
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.850
as in the table.

31
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.247
On long strings

32
00:01:19.313 --> 00:01:21.407
Edlib cuts the query into blocks

33
00:01:21.473 --> 00:01:23.633
of sixty-four rows and passes the

34
00:01:23.698 --> 00:01:25.400
carry from block to block.

35
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.294
A column of a thirty-thousand-base query

36
00:01:28.357 --> 00:01:30.710
takes four hundred sixty-nine blocks.

37
00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.690
The band skips cells altogether.

38
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.015
A path through a cell must make up the gap between

39
00:01:37.075 --> 00:01:39.217
its row and its column to get there

40
00:01:39.217 --> 00:01:41.240
and another gap to reach the end.

41
00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:43.470
If the distance is at most k

42
00:01:43.535 --> 00:01:45.740
any cell where the two gaps add up

43
00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:47.750
to more than k can be skipped.

44
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.357
What is left is a band around the diagonal

45
00:01:51.357 --> 00:01:53.780
an idea of Ukkonen's from 1985.

46
00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.963
Edlib does not know the distance in advance

47
00:01:56.963 --> 00:01:59.088
so it tries k equals sixty-four

48
00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:00.633
and doubles k until the

49
00:02:00.698 --> 00:02:02.050
band holds an answer.

50
00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.230
Pause and predict.

51
00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.404
At sixty percent similarity

52
00:02:06.404 --> 00:02:09.140
the distance is three hundred fifty-five.

53
00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.890
How many tries does the doubling need?

54
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:16.160
Four.

55
00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:17.227
Sixty-four

56
00:02:17.293 --> 00:02:19.901
one hundred twenty-eight and two hundred

57
00:02:19.966 --> 00:02:21.531
fifty-six are too small

58
00:02:21.531 --> 00:02:24.270
and five hundred twelve holds the answer.

59
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.330
Together the tries compute

60
00:02:26.396 --> 00:02:27.580
most of the table.

61
00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.870
At ninety-nine percent, one try is enough

62
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.690
about sixty-four thousand

63
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.090
cells of a million.

64
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.669
So: a column is a vector of steps

65
00:02:37.669 --> 00:02:39.506
kept in two bit vectors

66
00:02:39.506 --> 00:02:41.490
sixty-four rows to a word.

67
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.608
The band skips every cell that no path

68
00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:46.720
of cost at most k can reach.

69
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.791
And path recovery already

70
00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.751
knows the distance, so k can be set to it

71
00:02:51.751 --> 00:02:52.990
with no doubling.

72
00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.877
Next, how to recover the path without

73
00:02:55.946 --> 00:02:57.180
storing the table.
